{"files": [{"id": 27381617, "name": "Metadata - Hossie et al 2021 Sci Reports.pdf", "size": 107269, "is_link_only": false, "download_url": "https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/27381617", "supplied_md5": "6c1f92cfc733cf32cfa9ae9901d42e05", "computed_md5": "6c1f92cfc733cf32cfa9ae9901d42e05", "mimetype": "application/pdf"}, {"id": 27381623, "name": "toad_palatability.csv", "size": 2726, "is_link_only": false, "download_url": "https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/27381623", "supplied_md5": "5d646d5e32068a157037c8d00c0a918b", "computed_md5": "5d646d5e32068a157037c8d00c0a918b", "mimetype": "text/plain"}, {"id": 27381620, "name": "multiprey_L_1h.csv", "size": 4694, "is_link_only": false, "download_url": "https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/27381620", "supplied_md5": "ade6e942711e82af39222d85a0b872de", "computed_md5": "ade6e942711e82af39222d85a0b872de", "mimetype": "text/plain"}, {"id": 27381626, "name": "multiprey_T_1h.csv", "size": 4157, "is_link_only": false, "download_url": "https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/27381626", "supplied_md5": "0c21b993eb68b5d9de6681d8d8a418bc", "computed_md5": "0c21b993eb68b5d9de6681d8d8a418bc", "mimetype": "text/plain"}, {"id": 27381614, "name": "multiprey_PreyPreference.csv", "size": 8071, "is_link_only": false, "download_url": "https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/27381614", "supplied_md5": "b1443f78f35b81694f6b2b58fa650c39", "computed_md5": "b1443f78f35b81694f6b2b58fa650c39", "mimetype": "text/plain"}, {"id": 27381629, "name": "Multi-prey_SurplusKilling.csv", "size": 7624, "is_link_only": false, "download_url": "https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/27381629", "supplied_md5": "b7008bbf4c854c015614635e8354a1e3", "computed_md5": "b7008bbf4c854c015614635e8354a1e3", "mimetype": "text/plain"}], "folder_structure": {}, "authors": [{"id": 3812086, "full_name": "Thomas Hossie", "first_name": "Thomas", "last_name": "Hossie", "is_active": true, "url_name": "_", "orcid_id": ""}, {"id": 76816, "full_name": "Kevin Chan", "first_name": "Kevin", "last_name": "Chan", "is_active": false, "url_name": "Kevin_Chan", "orcid_id": ""}, {"id": 112638, "full_name": "Dennis L. Murray", "first_name": "Dennis", "last_name": "L. Murray", "is_active": false, "url_name": "_", "orcid_id": ""}], "custom_fields": [], "figshare_url": "https://figshare.com/articles/dataset/Data_for_Increasing_availability_of_palatable_prey_induces_predator_dependence_and_increases_predation_on_unpalatable_prey_/14346557", "download_disabled": false, "description": "Data sets use in Hossie et al. 2021 \u201cIncreasing availability of palatable prey induces predator\u2011dependence and increases predation on unpalatable prey\u201d Scientific Reports 11:6763 https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-021-86080-x<div><br></div><div>Abstract:</div><div><p>Understanding\nthe factors governing predation remains a top priority in ecology. Using a\ndragonfly nymph-tadpole system, we experimentally varied predator density, prey\ndensity, and prey species ratio to investigate: (i) whether predator\ninterference varies between prey types that differ in palatability, (ii)\nwhether adding alternate prey influences the magnitude of predator\ninterference, and (iii) whether patterns of prey selection vary according to\nthe predictions of optimal diet theory. In single-prey foraging trials,\npredation of palatable leopard frog tadpoles was limited by prey availability\nand predator interference, whereas predation of unpalatable toad tadpoles was\nlimited by handling time. Adding unpalatable prey did not affect the predator\u2019s\nkill rate of palatable prey, but the presence of palatable prey increased the\ninfluence of predator density on the kill rate of unpalatable prey and reduced\nunpalatable prey handling time. Prey selection did not change with shifts in\nthe relative abundance of prey types. Instead, predators selected\neasy-to-capture unpalatable prey at low total densities and harder-to-capture\npalatable prey at high densities. These results improve our understanding of\ngeneralist predation in communities with mobile prey, and illustrate that\ncharacteristics of the prey types involved govern the extent to which alternate\nprey influence the predator\u2019s kill rate.</p></div>", "funding": null, "funding_list": [], "version": 1, "status": "public", "size": 134541, "created_date": "2021-03-31T14:54:51Z", "modified_date": "2023-05-31T03:35:12Z", "is_public": true, "is_confidential": false, "is_metadata_record": false, "confidential_reason": "", "metadata_reason": "", "license": {"value": 1, "name": "CC BY 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"}, "tags": ["Functional Response", "functional response, ratio-dependent", "Functional response comparisons", "Predator-prey experiment", "palatability", "tadpoles", "toads", "Predator density", "Predator dependence", "sigmoidal", "optimal diet choice", "Optimal diet theory", "Apparent competition", "Animal Behaviour", "Ecology not elsewhere classified", "Community Ecology (excl. Invasive Species Ecology)", "Population Ecology", "Freshwater Ecology", "Behavioural Ecology"], "keywords": ["Functional Response", "functional response, ratio-dependent", "Functional response comparisons", "Predator-prey experiment", "palatability", "tadpoles", "toads", "Predator density", "Predator dependence", "sigmoidal", "optimal diet choice", "Optimal diet theory", "Apparent competition", "Animal Behaviour", "Ecology not elsewhere classified", "Community Ecology (excl. Invasive Species Ecology)", "Population Ecology", "Freshwater Ecology", "Behavioural Ecology"], "categories": [{"id": 24400, "title": "Animal behaviour", "parent_id": 24397, "path": "/24130/24397/24400", "source_id": "310901", "taxonomy_id": 100}, {"id": 24238, "title": "Ecology not elsewhere classified", "parent_id": 24211, "path": "/24130/24211/24238", "source_id": "310399", "taxonomy_id": 100}, {"id": 24217, "title": "Community ecology (excl. invasive species ecology)", "parent_id": 24211, "path": "